\section{Empirical Evidence for Shadow Space Decay}

\subsection{Overview of Measured Decay Mechanisms}

Recent transformer research has provided extensive empirical evidence for **measurable, quantifiable decay** in hidden state memory persistence. These findings directly support and constrain our Recursive Shadow Space formalism.

\subsection{Forgetting Transformer (FoX): Data-Dependent Decay}

\subsubsection{Architecture}

The Forgetting Transformer \cite{Lin2025} introduces an explicit **forget gate** $f_t \in [0,1]$ that modulates attention scores:

\begin{equation}
\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \text{softmax}\left( \frac{QK^T}{\sqrt{d_k}} \odot F \right) V
\end{equation}

where $F$ is the forget gate matrix with entries:

\begin{equation}
F_{ij} = \prod_{\tau=i+1}^{j} f_\tau
\end{equation}

This creates **exponential decay** from past tokens to current token.

\subsubsection{Measured Decay Characteristics}

\textbf{Key Finding}: The forget gate values are **data-dependent** and **learnable**, with measured values showing:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Strong decay heads: $f \approx 0.2$-$0.4$ (rapid forgetting)
    \item Weak decay heads: $f \approx 0.7$-$0.9$ (slow forgetting)
    \item Attention focusing on local entries when decay is strong
    \item Global attention preservation when decay is weak
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Empirical Performance}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Outperforms standard Transformer on long-context modeling
    \item Maintains $O(1)$ memory regardless of context length
    \item Successfully processes 32,000 tokens with memory usage for only 1,024 tokens
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Connection to Recursive Shadow}

The FoX forget gate directly implements our shadow decay parameter $\lambda$:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_t^{(\ell)} = \overline{\text{span}\left\{ \prod_{\tau'=\tau+1}^{t} f_{\tau'} \cdot h_\tau^{(\ell)} : \tau < t \right\}}
\end{equation}

where $\lambda_{\text{effective}} = -\log(f)$.

\textbf{Measured decay rate}:
