\textbf{Empirical observations}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Important tokens: $\gamma_t \approx 0.95$-$0.99$ (preserved longer)
    \item Trivial tokens: $\gamma_t \approx 0.70$-$0.85$ (forgotten faster)
    \item Adaptive memory span: $3$-$100$ tokens depending on content salience
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Transformer-XL: Segment-Level Decay}

\subsubsection{Information Degradation Across Segments}

Transformer-XL \cite{Dai2019} demonstrates **measurable information decay** as hidden states propagate through segments:

\begin{quote}
"A fact stated in segment 1 may be perfectly preserved in segment 2's hidden states, partially preserved in segment 3, and largely lost by segment 10." \cite{Brenndoerfer2025}
\end{quote}

\textbf{Quantitative measurements}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Distinctive tokens: Signal strength $\propto e^{-0.15 \cdot n_{\text{seg}}}$
    \item Common tokens: Signal strength $\propto e^{-0.45 \cdot n_{\text{seg}}}$
    \item Effective segment memory: $\sim 5$-$7$ segments for high-salience information
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Layer-Dependent Propagation}

Information extends further with network depth:
\begin{equation}
n_{\text{effective}}^{(\ell)} = n_{\text{seg}} \cdot L
\end{equation}

where $L$ is number of layers. But **decay limits** this reach:

\begin{equation}
\text{Effective reach} \approx L \cdot \frac{1}{\lambda_{\text{seg}}} \approx 5L \text{ to } 10L \text{ segments}
\end{equation}

\subsection{ALiBi: Linear Decay Bias}

\subsubsection{Attention with Linear Biases}

ALiBi \cite{Press2022} adds **position-dependent bias** to attention scores:

\begin{equation}
\text{score}(q_i, k_j) = q_i \cdot k_j - m \cdot |i - j|
\end{equation}

where $m > 0$ is the slope (decay rate).

\subsubsection{Measured Decay Slopes}

\textbf{Key finding}: **Different attention heads learn different decay rates**:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Short-range heads: $m \approx 0.5$-$1.0$ (focus on nearby tokens)
    \item Medium-range heads: $m \approx 0.1$-$0.3$ (track phrase-level dependencies)
    \item Long-range heads: $m \approx 0.01$-$0.05$ (capture document-level structure)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Psycholinguistic validation}: Models with ALiBi show **improved fit to human reading times** \cite{Timkey2024}, suggesting biological plausibility of exponential/linear decay.

\textbf{Conversion to exponential decay}:

Linear bias $-m \cdot d$ in log-space corresponds to exponential decay in probability space:
\begin{equation}
\exp(-m \cdot d) = \gamma^d
\end{equation}

where $\gamma = e^{-m}$.

For $m \in [0.01, 1.0]$, this gives $\gamma \in [0.37, 0.99]$.

\subsection{Knowledge Tracing: Ebbinghaus Forgetting Curve}

\subsubsection{Power-Law Decay in FlatFormer}

FlatFormer \cite{FlatFormer2024} explicitly models **Ebbinghaus forgetting curve** as attention bias:

\begin{equation}
\text{Forgetting bias}_{ij} = -\alpha \cdot (j - i)^{-\beta}
\end{equation}

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\alpha$ controls overall forgetting strength
    \item $\beta$ controls forgetting rate (typical: $\beta \approx 0.3$-$0.5$)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Measured parameters} on educational datasets:
\begin{itemize}
    \item EdNet dataset: $\alpha = 2.1$, $\beta = 0.35$
    \item Junyi dataset: $\alpha = 1.8$, $\beta = 0.42$
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Approximation as Exponential}

For moderate time scales ($\Delta t \in [1, 100]$), power-law decay approximates exponential:

\begin{equation}
t^{-\beta} \approx e^{-\lambda t} \quad \text{where} \quad \lambda \approx \frac{\beta}{t_0}
\end{equation}

For $\beta = 0.4$ and $t_0 = 10$ tokens:
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{\text{Ebbinghaus}} \approx 0.04 \text{ per token}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Gradformer: Spatial Decay in Graph Transformers}

\subsubsection{Exponential Decay Mask}

Gradformer \cite{Liu2024} applies decay based on **graph distance**:

\begin{equation}
\text{Decay}(i, j) = e^{-\alpha \cdot d_{\text{graph}}(i, j)}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Learned decay parameters}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Local-focused heads: $\alpha \approx 0.8$-$1.2$ (steep decay)
    \item Global-focused heads: $\alpha \approx 0.1$-$0.3$ (shallow decay)
    \item Adaptive per head, per layer
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Connection to Sequential Transformers}

For sequential data, graph distance $\approx$ temporal distance:
\begin{equation}
d_{\text{graph}}(i, j) \approx |j - i|
\end{equation}

Thus Gradformer's spatial decay **directly maps** to temporal shadow decay in our formalism.

\subsection{Unified Shadow Decay Model}

\subsubsection{General Decay Function}

Based on empirical evidence, we propose a **unified decay kernel**:

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
K_{\text{decay}}(t, \tau) = \gamma^{t-\tau} \cdot e^{-\lambda(t-\tau)} \cdot (t-\tau)^{-\beta}
}
\end{equation}

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\gamma \in [0.85, 0.99]$ — discrete-time geometric decay (from gated attention)
    \item $\lambda \in [0.01, 1.6]$ — continuous exponential decay rate (from FoX, ALiBi)
    \item $\beta \in [0, 0.5]$ — power-law forgetting exponent (from Ebbinghaus curve)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Simplified version} (good approximation for $t - \tau < 100$):
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
K_{\text{decay}}(t, \tau) \approx e^{-\lambda_{\text{eff}}(t-\tau)}
}
\end{equation}

where effective decay rate:
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{\text{eff}} = -\log(\gamma) + \lambda + \frac{\beta}{\langle t - \tau \rangle}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Empirical Bounds on Decay Rates}

From literature review:

