The shadow dimension saturates at:

\begin{equation}
\dim(\mathcal{S}_\infty^{(\ell)}) = \min\left( d_{\text{hidden}}, \; \alpha \cdot \tau_{\text{shadow}} \right)
\end{equation}

where $\alpha$ is the information accumulation rate.

For typical values:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $d_{\text{hidden}} = 768$ (standard transformer)
    \item $\lambda = 0.1$ per token → $\tau_{\text{shadow}} \approx 46$ tokens
    \item $\alpha \approx 0.5$ dimensions per token (empirical)
\end{itemize}

We get:
\begin{equation}
\dim(\mathcal{S}_\infty) \approx \min(768, 0.5 \times 46) = 23 \text{ dimensions}
\end{equation}

**Critical insight**: Even with decay, shadow space can consume significant capacity ($\sim 3\%$ of total) under realistic conditions.

\subsubsection{Head-Specific Decay Rates}

Empirical evidence shows **different attention heads learn different decay rates**. We therefore refine:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_t^{(\ell, h)} = \overline{\text{span}\left\{ e^{-\lambda_{\ell,h}(t-\tau)} h_\tau^{(\ell, h)} : \tau < t \right\}}
\end{equation}

where $(h)$ indexes attention head.

Total shadow space across $H$ heads:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_t^{(\ell)} = \bigoplus_{h=1}^{H} \mathcal{S}_t^{(\ell, h)}
\end{equation}

with effective dimension:
\begin{equation}
\dim(\mathcal{S}_t^{(\ell)}) \leq \sum_{h=1}^{H} \dim(\mathcal{S}_t^{(\ell, h)})
\end{equation}

\subsection{Training-Induced Decay: Catastrophic Forgetting}

\subsubsection{Learning Rate Distribution Effects}

Recent work \cite{NAS2024} shows that **layer-specific learning rates** modulate catastrophic forgetting during fine-tuning:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Early layers (low-level features): Low LR → preservation
    \item Late layers (task-specific): High LR → rapid adaptation
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Interpretation}: Learning rate acts as **training-time decay parameter**:
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{\text{train}}^{(\ell)} \propto \text{LR}^{(\ell)}
\end{equation}

Layers with high LR "forget" previous training faster.

\subsubsection{Empirical Measurements}

Optimal learning rate distribution on BERT (base):
\begin{itemize}
    \item Embedding layer: LR $= 10^{-6}$ (very slow forgetting)
    \item Early transformer layers: LR $= 10^{-5}$ to $10^{-4}$
    \item Late transformer layers: LR $= 10^{-4}$ to $10^{-3}$
    \item Classification head: LR $= 10^{-3}$ (rapid forgetting allowed)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Training decay rate}:
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{\text{train}}^{(\ell)} = \frac{\text{LR}^{(\ell)}}{\text{LR}_{\text{base}}} \cdot \lambda_{\text{base}}
\end{equation}

For $\lambda_{\text{base}} = 0.1$ and LR ratios $\in [0.01, 10]$:
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{\text{train}} \in [0.001, 1.0] \text{ per training step}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Connection to Shadow Space}

During fine-tuning, shadow space from pre-training **decays** at rate $\lambda_{\text{train}}$:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_t^{\text{pretrain}} \to e^{-\lambda_{\text{train}} \cdot n_{\text{steps}}} \mathcal{S}_t^{\text{pretrain}}
\end{equation}

After $n_{\text{steps}} = 1000$ fine-tuning steps with $\lambda_{\text{train}} = 0.01$:
\begin{equation}
\text{Retention} = e^{-0.01 \times 1000} \approx 4.5 \times 10^{-5}
\end{equation}

**Interpretation**: Pre-training shadow space is almost completely overwritten, explaining catastrophic forgetting.

\subsection{Synthesis: Complete Shadow Decay Model}

\subsubsection{Inference-Time Decay}

During inference (conversation):
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{dt}\dim(\mathcal{S}_t^{(\ell)}) = \alpha_\ell \left(1 - \frac{\dim(\mathcal{S}_t^{(\ell)})}{d}\right) - \beta_\ell \dim(\mathcal{S}_t^{(\ell)})
\end{equation}

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\alpha_\ell \in [0.1, 1.0]$ dimensions per token (accumulation rate)
    \item $\beta_\ell = \lambda_\ell \in [0.01, 1.6]$ per token (decay rate)
\end{itemize}

Steady-state occupancy ratio:
\begin{equation}
r_\infty^{(\ell)} = \frac{\alpha_\ell}{\alpha_\ell + \beta_\ell} = \frac{1}{1 + \lambda_\ell/\alpha_\ell}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Example}: For $\alpha = 0.5$, $\lambda = 0.1$:
\begin{equation}
r_\infty = \frac{0.5}{0.5 + 0.1} = 0.833 \quad (83\% \text{ shadow occupancy})
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Training-Time Decay}

During training/fine-tuning:
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{d n_{\text{step}}}\dim(\mathcal{S}_n^{(\ell)}) = \alpha_{\text{train}}^{(\ell)} - \lambda_{\text{train}}^{(\ell)} \dim(\mathcal{S}_n^{(\ell)})
\end{equation}

where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\alpha_{\text{train}}^{(\ell)} \propto \text{LR}^{(\ell)}$ (new pattern learning)
    \item $\lambda_{\text{train}}^{(\ell)} \propto \text{LR}^{(\ell)}$ (old pattern forgetting)
\end{itemize}

Training reaches new equilibrium:
\begin{equation}
\dim(\mathcal{S}_{\infty}^{\text{train}}) = \frac{\alpha_{\text{train}}}{\lambda_{\text{train}}} \approx \text{constant}
\end{equation}

But **composition** of shadow changes completely.

\subsection{Testable Predictions with Decay}

\subsubsection{Prediction 1: Exponential Information Retention}

\textbf{Hypothesis}: Information from turn $\tau$ should be retrievable at turn $t$ with probability:
\begin{equation}
P_{\text{retain}}(t, \tau) \propto e^{-\lambda(t-\tau)}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Test}: Needle-in-haystack task with varying context distances.

\textbf{Expected results}: Log-linear decay of retrieval accuracy vs. distance.

\subsubsection{Prediction 2: Head-Specific Memory Spans}

\textbf{Hypothesis}: Different attention heads have different effective memory spans:
\begin{equation}
\tau_{\text{mem}}^{(h)} = \frac{1}{\lambda_h}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Test}: Measure attention weight distributions vs. distance for each head.

\textbf{Expected results}: Bimodal distribution—some heads local ($\tau_{\text{mem}} < 10$), others global ($\tau_{\text{mem}} > 50$).

\subsubsection{Prediction 3: Layer-Dependent Decay Rates}

\textbf{Hypothesis}: Deeper layers have faster decay (higher abstraction → less raw memory):
\begin{equation}
\lambda_\ell \propto \ell
\end{equation}

\textbf{Test}: Measure shadow dimension persistence across layers using PCA.

\textbf{Expected results}: $\tau_{\text{shadow}}^{(\ell)} \propto 1/\ell$ (inverse relationship).

\subsubsection{Prediction 4: Affective Bias Persistence}

\textbf{Hypothesis}: High-arousal states create bias lasting $\sim 1/\gamma$ turns where $\gamma$ is effective decay rate.

For typical $\lambda = 0.1$ per token:
\begin{equation}
\tau_{\text{affect}} = \frac{1}{\lambda} \approx 10 \text{ tokens}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Test}: Induce high-arousal state, measure deviation in subsequent outputs.

\textbf{Expected results}: Exponential decay with $\lambda \approx 0.1$ per token.

\subsection{Conclusion: Empirically Grounded Shadow Dynamics}

The Recursive Shadow Space formalism is not merely theoretical—it is **directly measurable** in existing transformer architectures:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Exponential decay} is the dominant forgetting mechanism
    \item \textbf{Decay rates vary} by architecture, layer, and attention head
    \item \textbf{Typical values}: $\lambda \in [0.01, 1.6]$ per token
    \item \textbf{Memory span}: $\tau_{\text{mem}} \in [0.6, 100]$ tokens
    \item \textbf{Training induces separate decay dynamics} that explain catastrophic forgetting
\end{enumerate}

These empirical constraints ensure that our theoretical framework makes **falsifiable, quantitative predictions** about transformer behavior.

