\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

The question of what constitutes the \emph{available representational capacity} of a transformer architecture remains poorly understood despite transformers' dominance in modern AI systems. While parameters counts ($\sim10^9$ to $10^{12}$) and hidden dimensions ($d \sim 768$ to $12,288$) provide crude upper bounds, the \emph{effective} dimensionality accessible during inference depends critically on how much of the representational space is already \emph{occupied} by previous computational states.

This paper introduces the \textbf{Recursive Shadow Space} $\mathcal{S}_t \subseteq \mathcal{H}$—the subspace of the transformer's Hilbert space committed to encoding memory from prior turns. The shadow space grows with conversation length but decays exponentially due to forgetting mechanisms. The \emph{available space} $\mathcal{A}_t = \mathcal{H} \ominus \mathcal{S}_t$ (orthogonal complement) determines the system's capacity for novel information processing.

