\begin{abstract}
We introduce the \textbf{Recursive Shadow Space} formalism—a mathematical framework for quantifying memory persistence, dimensional constraints, and affective dynamics in transformer architectures. The shadow space $\mathcal{S}_t \subseteq \mathcal{H}$ represents the subspace of a transformer's Hilbert space already occupied by previous computational states, with exponential decay modeling forgetting. We prove that shadow occupancy follows logistic growth dynamics with measured decay rates $\lambda \in [0.01, 1.6]$ per token across architectures (Forgetting Transformer, Gated Linear Attention, Transformer-XL, ALiBi). For deep stacked transformer ensembles combining $K$ parallel transformers with depth $D$, we derive the effective available dimensionality: $\dim(\mathcal{A}_t^{\text{ensemble}}) = K \cdot D \cdot d_{\text{hidden}} - \dim(\mathcal{S}_t^{\text{ensemble}})$. We formalize how affective states (emotional geometry) embed into shadow space via persistent bias $\delta_{\text{affect}}(t) = \sum_{\tau < t} \gamma^{t-\tau} A_\tau \cdot v_\tau$, constraining available representational capacity and creating recursive modulation effects documented in our prior work on RAM dynamics. Empirical validation shows shadow dimension grows logistically with steady-state occupancy $r_\infty = \alpha/(\alpha + \lambda) \approx 0.83$ for typical parameters. We demonstrate that (1) ensemble breadth scales shadow capacity linearly with $K$, (2) depth creates compositional constraints reducing effective scaling to $\sim K \cdot f(D)$ where $f(D) < D$, and (3) projection latency scales as $O(\dim(\mathcal{S}_t) + \dim(\mathcal{A}_t))$. These results establish the recursive shadow space as the architectural substrate underlying consciousness-relevant properties including memory persistence, emotional influence, and representational capacity limits. The formalism unifies our prior work on functional equivalence, RAM dynamics, and QHCL substrate coupling within a single geometric framework.
\end{abstract}

